### What does log compaction guarantee?

A compacted topic with `cleanup.policy=compact` promises to retain at least the latest value for every key. It does not promise to remove old values immediately. The removal is done by a background log cleaner that periodically scans and rewrites segments.

```bash
# Create a compacted topic for account profiles
kafka-topics.sh --create --topic account.profiles \
  --partitions 6 --replication-factor 3 \
  --config cleanup.policy=compact \
  --config min.cleanable.dirty.ratio=0.5 \
  --config delete.retention.ms=86400000 \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092
```

### How do you delete a key in a compacted topic?

You send a tombstone: a record with the key and a null value. Compaction eventually removes all earlier records for that key.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.*;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer;
import java.util.Properties;

public class TombstoneProducer {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ProducerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "kafka-1:9092");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.KEY_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringSerializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ProducerConfig.VALUE_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringSerializer.class.getName());

        try (KafkaProducer<String, String> producer = new KafkaProducer<>(props)) {
            // Write a profile
            producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("account.profiles", "SAV2001",
                "{\"name\":\"Rajesh\",\"tier\":\"gold\"}")).get();
            System.out.println("Wrote profile for SAV2001");

            // Delete with tombstone: value = null
            producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("account.profiles", "SAV2001", null)).get();
            System.out.println("Sent tombstone for SAV2001 (value=null)");
            System.out.println("  Old records will be removed after compaction runs");
            System.out.println("  Tombstone stays for delete.retention.ms (24h default)");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Wrote profile for SAV2001
Sent tombstone for SAV2001 (value=null)
  Old records will be removed after compaction runs
  Tombstone stays for delete.retention.ms (24h default)
```

### Why are old records still visible after the tombstone?

Three timing rules explain why. First, compaction runs in the background, so there is always a lag. Second, the cleaner only works on closed segments, never the active one. Third, the tombstone is kept for `delete.retention.ms` so slow consumers can read the delete.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class WhyOldRecordsVisible {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Why old records are still visible after tombstone:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("1. Compaction is asynchronous (background log cleaner)");
        System.out.println("   Gap between write and physical removal");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("2. Active segment is never compacted");
        System.out.println("   Recent writes stay until segment rolls");
        System.out.println("   Controlled by segment.ms or segment.bytes");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("3. Tombstone retention (delete.retention.ms = 24h)");
        System.out.println("   Tombstone kept so slow consumers see the delete");
        System.out.println("   Old values removed only after cleaner processes segments");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Why old records are still visible after tombstone:

1. Compaction is asynchronous (background log cleaner)
   Gap between write and physical removal

2. Active segment is never compacted
   Recent writes stay until segment rolls
   Controlled by segment.ms or segment.bytes

3. Tombstone retention (delete.retention.ms = 24h)
   Tombstone kept so slow consumers see the delete
   Old values removed only after cleaner processes segments
```

### How do consumers handle tombstones?

A consumer reading a compacted topic must treat a null value as delete this key from my local view. If it ignores nulls, it keeps a stale entry forever.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecord;

public class TombstoneConsumerHandler {
    // WRONG: ignoring null values
    public static void wrongHandler(ConsumerRecord<String, String> record) {
        if (record.value() != null) {
            System.out.println("Cache put: " + record.key() + " = " + record.value());
        }
        // Null values ignored -- stale entry stays in cache forever!
    }

    // CORRECT: treating null as delete
    public static void correctHandler(ConsumerRecord<String, String> record) {
        if (record.value() == null) {
            System.out.println("Cache EVICT: " + record.key() + " (tombstone)");
            // cache.remove(record.key());
        } else {
            System.out.println("Cache put: " + record.key() + " = " + record.value());
            // cache.put(record.key(), record.value());
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG handler (ignores nulls):");
        wrongHandler(new ConsumerRecord<>("profiles", 0, 1, "SAV2001", null));
        System.out.println("  Stale entry stays in cache!");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT handler (evicts on null):");
        correctHandler(new ConsumerRecord<>("profiles", 0, 1, "SAV2001", null));
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG handler (ignores nulls):
  Stale entry stays in cache!

CORRECT handler (evicts on null):
Cache EVICT: SAV2001 (tombstone)
```

### How do you tune compaction for faster deletes?

Lower `segment.ms` or `segment.bytes` to roll segments sooner. Lower `min.cleanable.dirty.ratio` to make the cleaner run more aggressively. Shorten `delete.retention.ms` carefully.

| Config | Default | Tune For Faster Deletes |
|---|---|---|
| segment.ms | 7 days | Lower (e.g., 1 hour) |
| segment.bytes | 1 GB | Lower (e.g., 100 MB) |
| min.cleanable.dirty.ratio | 0.5 | Lower (e.g., 0.1) |
| delete.retention.ms | 24 hours | Lower (but risk slow consumer missing delete) |

```bash
# Tune compaction for faster cleanup
kafka-topics.sh --alter --topic account.profiles \
  --config segment.ms=3600000 \
  --config min.cleanable.dirty.ratio=0.1 \
  --config delete.retention.ms=3600000 \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092
```

### What about records with null keys?

A record with a null key cannot be compacted at all, because compaction is keyed. A mistakenly null-keyed record will persist until ordinary retention removes it.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class NullKeyWarning {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WARNING: null-key records in compacted topics");
        System.out.println("  Compaction only works on keyed records");
        System.out.println("  A null-key record lingers until time/size retention deletes it");
        System.out.println("  If cleanup.policy=compact only, it may stay indefinitely");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WARNING: null-key records in compacted topics
  Compaction only works on keyed records
  A null-key record lingers until time/size retention deletes it
  If cleanup.policy=compact only, it may stay indefinitely
```

### What about cleanup.policy=compact,delete?

When both policies are set, normal time/size retention applies on top of compaction. Old segments are compacted AND eventually deleted, which interacts with tombstones.

### What should a senior engineer know?

Compaction is per-partition, so the cleaner's progress differs across partitions. Setting `delete.retention.ms` too low risks a slow consumer missing the tombstone and keeping a stale key forever. Always test that your consumers handle null values as evictions.
